<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================================
  CloudLib PMD 7 gate — WARN ruleset (pmdAdvisory).
  ============================================================================================

  Derived from the authoritative rule catalogue pmd-evidence/style-extraction/_catalog.md
  (workspace root of CloudMods-Inworld). The catalogue, not this file, decides what is in here.

  WHY THIS IS SPLIT IN TWO FILES
    Gradle's `Pmd` task fails the build on *any* violation; it has no notion of severity and
    `ignoreFailures` is all-or-nothing. The catalogue (section F-24) layers the rules into
    "error" (build fails) and "warn" (report only), so the layering is implemented as two
    rulesets driven by two tasks:
      * pmd/cloudlib-ruleset.xml               -> task `pmdMain`     -> ignoreFailures = false
      * pmd/cloudlib-warnings.xml (this file)  -> task `pmdAdvisory` -> ignoreFailures = true
    Both files ship inside the `cloudpmd` jar; the `dev.vfyjxf.cloudlib.pmd` plugin unpacks
    them into the consuming build (there is deliberately no second copy among its resources).

  WHAT BELONGS HERE
    Catalogue F-24: a rule whose current state is not clean, or was never measured, is a warning;
    only rules measured clean become errors. So this file holds exactly the rules that report
    against today's sources or cannot yet be trusted to fail a build, and it is the buffer from
    which rules graduate into the error ruleset once their existing hits are cleared.

  SCOPE
    Same as the error ruleset: `main` source sets only, file-level exclusions live in the Gradle
    plugin's task configuration. `pmdAdvisory` analyses exactly the same sources as `pmdMain`
    and reports to build/reports/pmd/advisory.{html,xml}.

  RULE REFERENCES
    As in the error ruleset: standard rules keep their PMD message and description, with an XML
    comment carrying the catalogue id and the reason.

  [implementation check] items resolved here:
    * NonStaticWildcardImport and OptionalOutsideApi are XPath rules (catalogue section B); all
      their attribute names were verified against real AST dumps of PMD 7.28.0:
      ImportDeclaration carries @Static, @ImportOnDemand and @ImportedName; PackageDeclaration
      carries @Name; ClassOrInterfaceType carries @SimpleName.
    * PMD 7.28.0 gained a standard rule `OnDemandImport` (category/java/bestpractices.xml,
      since 7.28.0) whose `allowStaticImportsFrom` / `allowTypeImportsFrom` properties can
      express the same "non-static wildcard only" policy that catalogue D-27 says AvoidStarImport
      cannot. It is deliberately NOT used: the catalogue defines B-02 as a section-B inline rule,
      and the inline rule has the narrower, fully-verified behaviour.
    * UnusedFormalParameter `checkAll` is a Boolean defaulting to false (private methods only);
      the catalogue's value is set explicitly.
    * DanglingJavadoc is in category/java/documentation.xml in 7.28.0, not in errorprone as the
      catalogue's table says. It is a warning because it was never measured (catalogue C-19).
    * C-04 FormalParameterNamingConventions, C-05 LocalVariableNamingConventions,
      C-06 TypeParameterNamingConventions, C-11 MissingOverride and B-04
      PublicNonFinalInstanceField briefly sat here: PMD 7.28.0 really does report them, contrary
      to the catalogue's "current state" column. Their sites have since been fixed (see the
      error ruleset's resolved item 15) and all five now run as errors in `pmdMain`.
    * S-2 UnusedAssignment, A-2 ConstructorCallsOverridableMethod and A-3
      AvoidReassigningLoopVariables are the three rules the sweep report
      (lint-tools-research/05-pmd-sweep.md, grade S/A) puts in the warning layer rather than the
      error one; their measured hit counts and the reason each stays a warning are on the rules
      themselves. The report's other S/A picks are errors in `pmdMain` (see the error ruleset).

  ============================================================================================
  THIRD SOURCE — pmd-evidence/framework-rules/_framework-catalog.md
  ============================================================================================
  The same framework contract the error ruleset draws on. Two of its sixteen rules stay warnings,
  because both still report against today's sources and both remedies are framework-side work
  rather than call-site discipline — the rule is not weak, the code it looks at is not yet fixed:
    * A-11 FrameworkIdMustBeRegistered — three `ExposeChannel.of(...)` declarations in the main
      sources have no `ExposeChannelRegistry.register(...)` in their file (catalogue E-5). They are
      latent rather than broken: each channel is served through a binding, which carries the channel
      itself, so the registry lookup only starts to matter once the channel is offered to a viewer
      that never asked for it. Wrapping them in `register(...)` is deliberately NOT done: every one
      of the three sites builds a fresh channel per call, and the registry throws
      IllegalArgumentException on a duplicate id, so the one-line wrap the catalogue suggests would
      turn a latent fault into a startup crash. The catalogue's own fix for this (its D-2.3) is a
      public declaration gate on CloudLib's side, so the rule stays a warning until that exists.
    * B-05 NoInternalPackageReference — one downstream import of `cloudlib.internal.StyleConfig`
      (NimbusConfig.java:5, catalogue E-6), because the public API has no equivalent for persisting
      the theme selection. The catalogue's remedy is again a public CloudLib entry point (its
      D-2.5). The finding is left visible on purpose: suppressing a warning whose fix is pending
      would hide the signal that the fix is pending.

  [implementation check] B-05's XPath is the only section-B rule in this file:
    * `ImportDeclaration/@ImportedName` holds the fully qualified name (already relied on by the
      `IllegalImport` rule in the error ruleset) and `PackageDeclaration/@Name` the package, both
      re-verified with `pmd ast-dump` on 7.28.0.
    * The catalogue asks for the rule to be excluded from the CloudLib project. It is expressed in
      XPath instead — "the compilation unit's own package is not `dev.vfyjxf.cloudlib.**`" — which
      is the same predicate without a per-build file exclusion, so the one ruleset keeps working
      for both projects and for third-party consumers. Verified: Nimbus reports the one known
      site, CloudLib reports none.
-->
<ruleset name="cloudlib-pmd-warnings"
         xmlns="http://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset/2.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset/2.0.0 https://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset_2_0_0.xsd">

    <description>
        CloudLib PMD 7 gate (warnings ruleset). Run as `pmdAdvisory`, which records violations
        but never fails the build. Derived from pmd-evidence/style-extraction/_catalog.md and,
        for the S/A picks, from lint-tools-research/05-pmd-sweep.md; the error layer lives in
        pmd/cloudlib-ruleset.xml and runs as `pmdMain`.
    </description>

    <!-- ======================================================================================
         B - inline XPath rules, severity warn
         ====================================================================================== -->

    <!-- B-02: `import a.b.*;` is a violation; `import static a.b.*;` is not. The catalogue's
         sketch is used verbatim — @Static and @ImportOnDemand on ImportDeclaration were
         verified with `pmd ast-dump` on 7.28.0. Seven non-static wildcards exist today in
         api/ui and api/plugin (catalogue E-18), hence a warning rather than an error.
         Catalogue F-03; D-27 explains why AvoidStarImport cannot be used instead. -->
    <rule name="NonStaticWildcardImport"
          language="java"
          message="Use explicit imports; only `import static a.b.*` may be on demand"
          class="net.sourceforge.pmd.lang.rule.xpath.XPathRule">
        <description>
            Reports on-demand (wildcard) imports that are not static. `import static a.b.*` is
            allowed.
        </description>
        <priority>3</priority>
        <properties>
            <property name="xpath">
                <value><![CDATA[
//ImportDeclaration[@Static = false() and @ImportOnDemand = true()]
]]></value>
            </property>
        </properties>
    </rule>

    <!-- B-03: java.util.Optional must not be used in the implementation packages
         (dev.vfyjxf.cloudlib.{ui,network,internal} and dev.vfyjxf.nimbus.{content,internal});
         the api subtrees are explicitly allowed, because the api layer returns Optional from
         12 files that are legitimate consumers of it. Only the import is checked; the optional
         type-use clause of the catalogue sketch is dropped because the type cannot be named by
         its simple name without that import, so the import clause already covers every use, and
         a fully qualified use is caught by A-03 NoFullyQualifiedNames anyway. (That sketch also
         names a node that does not exist in PMD 7: `ClassOrInterfaceType` was renamed
         `ClassType`.) Neither project has a hit today, so this is a warning that guards against
         regressions. Catalogue F-04. -->
    <rule name="OptionalOutsideApi"
          language="java"
          message="java.util.Optional is not allowed outside the api packages"
          class="net.sourceforge.pmd.lang.rule.xpath.XPathRule">
        <description>
            Reports `import java.util.Optional` inside dev.vfyjxf.cloudlib.{ui,network,internal}
            and dev.vfyjxf.nimbus.{content,internal}. The api packages are exempt.
        </description>
        <priority>3</priority>
        <properties>
            <property name="xpath">
                <value><![CDATA[
//ImportDeclaration[@ImportedName = 'java.util.Optional']
  [ancestor::CompilationUnit/PackageDeclaration
     [matches(@Name, '^(dev\.vfyjxf\.cloudlib\.(ui|network|internal)|dev\.vfyjxf\.nimbus\.(content|internal))')]]
]]></value>
            </property>
        </properties>
    </rule>

    <!-- ======================================================================================
         C - standard rules, severity warn
         ====================================================================================== -->

    <!-- C-12 bestpractices: single-abstract-method interfaces should carry @FunctionalInterface.
         At least six are missing the annotation today (catalogue E-19). -->
    <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/ImplicitFunctionalInterface"/>

    <!-- C-13 bestpractices: switches over enums should be exhaustive. Clean today. -->
    <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/NonExhaustiveSwitch"/>

    <!-- C-14 bestpractices: no unused private fields (0-2 expected). -->
    <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedPrivateField"/>

    <!-- C-15 bestpractices: no unused private methods (0-2 expected). -->
    <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedPrivateMethod"/>

    <!-- C-16 bestpractices: no unused local variables (0-2 expected). -->
    <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedLocalVariable"/>

    <!-- C-17 bestpractices: no unused formal parameters. checkAll stays false so that interface
         implementations and overrides, whose parameters are fixed by the signature, are not
         reported. -->
    <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedFormalParameter">
        <properties>
            <property name="checkAll" value="false"/>
        </properties>
    </rule>

    <!-- C-19 documentation: javadoc that belongs to nothing. Never measured, so a warning for
         now; if the first runs show zero hits the catalogue allows promoting it to the error
         ruleset. -->
    <rule ref="category/java/documentation.xml/DanglingJavadoc"/>

    <!-- C-22 design: a class with only static members must not be instantiable. About eleven
         CloudLib classes lack a private constructor (catalogue E-11..E-16) and BuiltinKeys is a
         deliberate exception, so this stays a warning. Catalogue F-06: the rule was called
         `UseUtilityClass` before PMD 7.27.0. -->
    <rule ref="category/java/design.xml/InstantiableUtilityClass"/>

    <!-- C-24 bestpractices: a caught exception must not be swallowed when wrapping it into
         another one. Expected clean; kept as a warning so an eventual regression is visible.
         The catalogue files this under errorprone, but 7.28.0 has it in bestpractices. -->
    <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/PreserveStackTrace"/>

    <!-- ======================================================================================
         Null-check rules — DEMOTED error -> warn (user directive 2026-09-28: external null
         annotations can be wrong, so a flagged check must always have a way to be kept).
         They run here through pmdAdvisory and never fail the build. Two escape hatches work
         out of the box, applied by PMD's reporting layer to every rule:
           * `// NOPMD` on the offending line;
           * `@SuppressWarnings("PMD.RedundantNullCheck")` / `@SuppressWarnings("PMD.DuplicateNullCheck")`
             on the enclosing declaration.
         Both are exercised by regression tests (see the rules' test classes).
         ====================================================================================== -->

    <!-- A-05: a null check whose checked expression is provably non-null is redundant, whatever
         the shape of the check: == / != (yoda included), Objects.isNull/nonNull, an assert
         condition, a ternary or compound condition, a `case null` label, and
         Objects.requireNonNull / Checks.checkNotNull applied to an already-provable expression.
         The proof itself is a fixed, conservative ladder — syntactic constants (this, super, new,
         literals, enum constants, concatenation, requireNonNull/checkNotNull/valueOf/getClass/
         Optional.orElseThrow, and orElse of a provable argument); locals whose declaration and
         every assignment in their method are provable; fields declared non-null in this file (by
         simple name or through this.); and calls that resolve to a same-file declaration that
         returns non-null and cannot be overridden from another file (private, static, final, or
         in a final/record/enum/anonymous type).
         EXEMPT BY DESIGN (miss-before-error, so no false positives): parameters, array accesses,
         foreach/resource/pattern/catch/lambda variables, lambdas and method references, @Nullable
         declarations, inherited and cross-file fields and methods, reads through a receiver other
         than this, library calls, unresolvable chains, and Optional.orElse of an unknown argument
         (it can return that null).
         Landed as error on 2026-09-28 and demoted to warn the same day: even the conservative
         ladder leans on in-file nullness declarations, and an annotation that is simply wrong
         (in this file or upstream) must not cost the build — the check stays as advice.
         依据 用户指令 2026-09-28：扫 null check / 判源 nullable / 禁冗余；同日降级 warn。 -->
    <rule name="RedundantNullCheck"
          language="java"
          message="Do not check an expression for null whose source is provably non-null"
          class="dev.vfyjxf.cloudlib.pmd.rules.RedundantNullCheckRule">
        <description>
            Reports every shape of null check whose checked expression the rule can prove
            non-null: a syntactic constant, a local assigned only from provable sources, a field
            declared non-null in the same file, or a same-file method declared to return non-null.
            Anything the ladder cannot prove is left alone. Suppress a wrong finding with
            `// NOPMD` or `@SuppressWarnings("PMD.RedundantNullCheck")`.
        </description>
        <priority>3</priority>
    </rule>

    <!-- A-06: a variable whose nullness is already established must not be null-checked again.
         Knowledge comes only from unconditional places: an executed
         Objects.requireNonNull(x) / Checks.checkNotNull(x) (statement, initializer, or assignment
         right-hand side), a guard `if (x == null) return/throw/break/continue;` and its
         `if (x != null) ... else leave;` mirror, a guard holding inside its then-branch, and the
         left null check of a condition pinning the variable for its right operand
         (x != null && x != null, x == null || x == null).
         EXEMPT BY DESIGN: branches merge by intersection, so a guard that holds in only one branch
         never survives the join; loops, switches and try blocks drop everything gained inside; an
         assignment anywhere in between clears the variable; assertions pin nothing (they may be
         disabled); only locals and parameters are tracked, because nothing outside the method can
         reassign those (fields and lambda bodies are deliberately out); and a labelled
         break/continue escaping a statement keeps only what was known before it.
         Demoted error -> warn together with A-05 (same directive): wrong upstream nullness can
         make a second check the only correct line of defense, so this reports, never blocks.
         依据 用户指令 2026-09-28：扫 null check / 判源 nullable / 禁冗余；同日降级 warn。 -->
    <rule name="DuplicateNullCheck"
          language="java"
          message="Do not check a variable for null whose nullness is already established"
          class="dev.vfyjxf.cloudlib.pmd.rules.DuplicateNullCheckRule">
        <description>
            Reports a null check (== / != / isNull / nonNull) on a local or parameter that a
            requireNonNull/checkNotNull call or a leaving null guard in the same method has
            already pinned down, with no assignment to the variable in between. Suppress a wrong
            finding with `// NOPMD` or `@SuppressWarnings("PMD.DuplicateNullCheck")`.
        </description>
        <priority>3</priority>
    </rule>

    <!-- ======================================================================================
         S/A - the three 05-pmd-sweep.md picks that stay warnings
         (lint-tools-research/05-pmd-sweep.md; ids below are that report's)
         ====================================================================================== -->

    <!-- S-2 bestpractices: a value stored and then overwritten or never read (dead store),
         including `this` and static fields. All 11 current hits are real leftovers from a
         refactor: SliderBlueprint's three field initialisers are overwritten immediately,
         InworldCoordinator's `zoneLayout`, CssValues' `aIndex` twice, Nimbus DashEnvelope /
         NameplateLayouter x2 / TargetDwell. It stays a warn because the rule's own
         documentation warns it can misfire on timing-sensitive concurrency, and both projects
         keep volatile hot-path snapshots (catalogue D-23) — the 11 must be read by a human
         before this could ever gate. Hits: CloudLib 7 + Nimbus 4. -->
    <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedAssignment"/>

    <!-- A-2 errorprone: a constructor calling an overridable method through `this` — the
         subclass's fields are still uninitialised at that point. The 31 CloudLib hits are all
         the Widget tree (ButtonWidget/ChipWidget/LabelWidget/ItemStripWidget/PartNode/
         ProgressBarWidget/RichTextWidget calling onMount/setFocusable/stylePart in their
         constructors), i.e. a systematic design of the UI layer rather than 31 slips, and
         removing them means deferring those calls to a post-mount lifecycle hook — an
         architecture change the gate should not force. Hits: CloudLib 31 + Nimbus 1
         (Nimbus.java:38). -->
    <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml/ConstructorCallsOverridableMethod"/>

    <!-- A-3 bestpractices: a loop variable reassigned inside the loop body, which makes the
         loop's bounds implicit and is the hardest off-by-one to read in the layout / grid
         routing algorithms. Hits: CloudLib 5 (CssTokenizer:93, Events:376,
         FloatingPositioning:109, LeaderGridRouter:729, VanillaHudSampler:147), Nimbus 0. A warn
         rather than an error because the reassignments are deliberate skip-a-step loops; the
         `foreachReassign`/`forReassign` properties are the dial if this is ever promoted. -->
    <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/AvoidReassigningLoopVariables"/>

    <!-- ======================================================================================
         FRAMEWORK - the two catalogue rules that stay warnings (severity warn)
         See the THIRD SOURCE block in the header for why each one is not an error yet.
         ====================================================================================== -->

    <!-- A-11 Framework: a declared expose channel or kind must be registered in the same file.
         Catalogue A-11. Per compilation unit, because that is the scope a PMD rule can see: an
         ExposeChannel.of / ExposeLayerChannel.of / ExposeKind.of call is fine when it is the
         argument of the matching registry's register(...), or when the file registers anything
         through that registry at all. Otherwise the declared identity is one nobody can resolve,
         and RuntimeExposeSession.channelOf finds no codec for it — serve() then returns false
         without a word (RuntimeExposeSession.java:277-290).
         Stock: 3 sites in CloudLib (BlockEntitySync.expose x2, BasicMenu.serve), 0 in Nimbus
         (catalogue E-5). See the header for why they stay. The escape hatch is the marker
         `framework-id-unregistered-ok` on the offending line, in the rule; PMD's own `// NOPMD`
         works as well. -->
    <rule name="FrameworkIdMustBeRegistered"
          language="java"
          message="A declared expose channel or kind must be registered in the same file"
          class="dev.vfyjxf.cloudlib.pmd.rules.FrameworkIdMustBeRegisteredRule">
        <description>
            Reports an ExposeChannel.of, ExposeLayerChannel.of or ExposeKind.of declaration in a
            compilation unit that never registers through the matching registry. Catalogue A-11.
        </description>
        <priority>3</priority>
    </rule>

    <!-- B-05 Framework: a consumer must not import dev.vfyjxf.cloudlib.internal.**.
         Catalogue B-05. The internal subtree is an implementation package with no annotation to
         mark it, so the boundary is the package name and only a consumer can cross it: CloudLib's
         own references to its internals are legitimate, which is what the package test in the
         XPath expresses.
         Stock: 1 site, NimbusConfig.java:5 (catalogue E-6) — the import exists because the public
         API has no way to persist the theme selection, so the fix belongs to CloudLib (catalogue
         D-2.5) and the finding stays visible until then. -->
    <rule name="NoInternalPackageReference"
          language="java"
          message="A consumer must not import dev.vfyjxf.cloudlib.internal"
          class="net.sourceforge.pmd.lang.rule.xpath.XPathRule">
        <description>
            Reports an import of dev.vfyjxf.cloudlib.internal.** from a compilation unit whose own
            package is not dev.vfyjxf.cloudlib.**; CloudLib's internal references are exempt.
            Catalogue B-05.
        </description>
        <priority>3</priority>
        <properties>
            <property name="xpath">
                <value><![CDATA[
//ImportDeclaration[starts-with(@ImportedName, 'dev.vfyjxf.cloudlib.internal.')]
  [not(ancestor::CompilationUnit/PackageDeclaration[starts-with(@Name, 'dev.vfyjxf.cloudlib')])]
]]></value>
            </property>
        </properties>
    </rule>

</ruleset>
